---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import Grade from '../components/Grade.astro';
import State from '../components/State.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import FundingChart from '../components/FundingChart.astro';
---
<Base
  terms
  title="Donor influence and introduced funding pathways"
  description="How $9.5 million reached two Harvard faculty members from donors Jeffrey Epstein introduced after Harvard stopped accepting his own gifts, with a comparison to MIT."
  eyebrow="Funding pathways"
  lede="Harvard refused Epstein’s own money after 2008. Between 2010 and 2015, donors he had introduced gave $9.5 million to the same two faculty members. This page sets out what is documented about that route and what is not."
  reviewed="2026-11-20"
  basis="Harvard’s 2020 report; MIT’s 2020 Goodwin Procter report"
>
  <h2 id="money">Where the documented money went</h2>
  <p>
    Every figure below comes from an institutional report or reporting on one, and all bars share one scale.
    The chart shows money that a record documents. It does not show influence, and it does not show that
    anyone directed a gift.
  </p>
  <FundingChart />

  <h2 id="pattern">The pattern</h2>
  <p>A gift can reach a university by three routes. Harvard’s rules after 2008 closed only the first.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap full">
    <table class="stack">
      <caption>Three routes, and what Harvard’s review found for each</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>After 2008</th><th>Evidence state</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Direct gift from Epstein</th><td data-label="After 2008">None accepted. The total of $9.1 million was all received between 1998 and 2008.</td><td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="verified" /></td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A1" /><Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Gift from a donor he introduced</th><td data-label="After 2008">$7.5 million for Professor Nowak’s work and $2 million for Professor Church’s, 2010 to 2015.</td><td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="verified" /></td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A1" /><Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Non-financial standing</th><td data-label="After 2008">Key card, office use and website presence at one program; meetings with faculty.</td><td data-label="Evidence state"><State s="verified" /></td><td data-label="Source"><Grade g="A1" /><Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The review recorded that the introduced donors denied Epstein had directed their gifts, and it found no evidence
    that he had. It also noted that his staff acted as intermediaries on some of them.<Cite id="harvard-2020-report" /><Cite id="crimson-2020-report" />
    An introduction is therefore documented. Direction of the money is not.
  </p>

  <h2 id="black">The Leon Black pathway</h2>
  <p>
    The clearest example in the record is the financier Leon Black. Reporting on the 2020 review attributes $7 million
    in unrestricted gifts for Professor Nowak’s work to Mr Black, his wife and their family foundation between 2011 and
    2015.<Cite id="axios-2020-black" /> That $7 million sits inside the review’s $7.5 million total for all introduced
    donors to Professor Nowak. A representative confirmed that Epstein introduced Mr Black to the research and said the
    funds were not provided by Epstein.<Cite id="axios-2020-black" />
  </p>
  <Callout title="Two figures of $7.5 million that are not the same thing">
    <p>
      The $7.5 million in Harvard’s review is the sum given by all introduced donors for Professor Nowak’s work.
      Separately, in 2018 the Debra and Leon Black Family Foundation gave $7.5 million to fund fellowships for veterans
      and serving military at Harvard Kennedy School and other Harvard schools.<Cite id="hks-2018-black" /> We found no
      public record connecting the fellowship gift to Epstein, and this site does not treat it as part of the
      introduced-donor pathway.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p>
    Reporting in 2026 from the Justice Department release described further contacts around donations, including a 2014
    biotechnology investment venture discussed between Epstein and Professor Church.<Cite id="cnn-2026-donations" /><Cite id="globe-2026-spotlight" />
    A June 2026 letter from the ranking member of the House Judiciary Committee asserts that Harvard development staff
    knew of and encouraged Epstein’s role in the Black gifts.<Cite id="raskin-2026" /> That is an assertion by the
    letter’s authors. Harvard has not published a finding on it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mit">Comparison: MIT Media Lab</h2>
  <p>
    MIT’s commissioned review, by the law firm Goodwin Procter, found a different route to a similar end. MIT did take
    Epstein’s money after his conviction: of ten gifts totalling $850,000 between 2002 and 2017, nine came after 2008,
    $525,000 to the Media Lab and $225,000 to Professor Seth Lloyd.<Cite id="mit-2020-report" /> Three vice-presidents
    learned of the gifts in 2013 and allowed them to continue on informal conditions, including
    anonymity.<Cite id="mit-2020-report" /><Cite id="techreview-2020-mit" /> He visited the campus nine times between
    2013 and 2017.<Cite id="mit-2020-report" />
  </p>
  <p>
    The Media Lab’s director, Joi Ito, resigned in September 2019. Professor Lloyd was placed on paid leave and later
    disciplined, and continued to teach.<Cite id="mit-2020-lloyd" /> In 2026, MIT’s student newspaper reported that
    the newly released emails pointed to donor connections the 2020 review had not covered.<Cite id="tech-2026-mit" />
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap full">
    <table class="stack">
      <caption>How the two institutions differed</caption>
      <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Harvard</th><th>MIT</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Direct gifts after 2008?</th><td data-label="Harvard">No</td><td data-label="MIT">Yes, nine gifts</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Introduced-donor gifts?</th><td data-label="Harvard">$9.5 million documented</td><td data-label="MIT">Outside the 2020 report’s findings; raised in 2026 reporting</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Who knew?</th><td data-label="Harvard">Review found no evidence that University leadership knew of the Program’s website page about him</td><td data-label="MIT">Three vice-presidents knew from 2013</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Physical access</th><td data-label="Harvard">Key card and office; more than 40 visits</td><td data-label="MIT">Nine visits, as a guest</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="why">Why this matters to a reader</h2>
  <p>
    For a prospective student or an alumnus, the useful question is how a university’s controls work in practice. In
    both cases a central rule or decision existed, and the relationship continued at the level of an individual
    laboratory. Harvard’s review responded by recommending clearer gift policies and donor vetting.<Cite id="bacow-2020" />
    Whether those changes are sufficient is a judgement this site leaves to the reader.
  </p>
</Base>
